 "stimulus_html": "<p>“Though a biography, and of course admitting of familiar anecdote, excursive digressions, and a flexible texture of narrative, yet, for the most part, it is essentially historic. Washington, in fact, had very little private life, but was eminently a public character. All his actions and concerns almost from boyhood were connected with the history of his country. In writing his biography, therefore, I am obliged to take glances over collateral history, as seen from his point of view and influencing his plans, . . .</p>\n<p>I have endeavored to execute my task with candor and fidelity; stating facts on what appeared to be good authority, and avoiding as much as possible all false coloring and exaggeration. My work is founded on the correspondence of Washington, which, in fact, affords the amplest and surest groundwork for his biography. This I have consulted as it exists in manuscript in the archives of the Department of State, to which I have had full and frequent access. I have also made frequent use of ‘Washington’s Writings,’ as published by Mr. Sparks; a careful collation of many of them with the originals having convinced me of the general correctness of the collection, and the safety with which it may be relied upon for historical purposes.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">—Washington Irving, 1855</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Irving, Washington. <i>The Life and Times of Washington.</i> New York: G.P. Putnam and Sons, J.B. Holland, 1876</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 615244,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "According to the author’s preface, what was his main purpose in writing this biography?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168948,
     "content_html": "To write a work of fiction with entertaining digressions"
    },
    {
     "id": 1168947,
     "content_html": "To retell familiar stories"
    },
    {
     "id": 1168949,
     "content_html": "To produce an accurate history"
    },
    {
     "id": 1168946,
     "content_html": "To justify collateral history"
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615245,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What steps did the author take to keep his work as true as possible to the actual events he described in the book?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168952,
     "content_html": "He had Martha Washington review early drafts of the book."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168953,
     "content_html": "He studied Washington’s letters and other writings."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168951,
     "content_html": "He plagiarized the work of Mr. Sparks."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168950,
     "content_html": "He searched for previously unknown collections of secondary sources."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615246,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Where did the author acquire many of the records he used for the book?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168956,
     "content_html": "He visited the Library of Congress in Washington, DC."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168957,
     "content_html": "He consulted materials in the archives of the Department of State."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168955,
     "content_html": "He visited Mount Vernon to interview Washington himself."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168954,
     "content_html": "He guarded his privacy and concentration by having research materials shipped to him at home."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 615247,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Why does the author say that Washington was “eminently a public character”?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1168961,
     "content_html": "Washington was always aware that everything he did would be carefully scrutinized."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168960,
     "content_html": "Washington spent his whole life trying to build his personal fortune."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168959,
     "content_html": "Washington refused to engage in personal correspondence but wrote only letters intended for the public."
    },
    {
     "id": 1168958,
     "content_html": "Washington sought vows of secrecy from those engaged in his actions and concerns."
    }
   ],
